  "period_note": "Year-end (31 December 2012) position from Southeast Bank's own audited annual report multi-year \"Key Financial Data and Ratios\" summary table. CONSOLIDATED (Group) basis. Southeast Bank prepares its multi-year \"Key Financial Data and Ratios\" summary tables on the consolidated basis, so no Solo/Standalone multi-year series exists in these reports; this was verified against the underlying statements (FY2025 total assets 554,424.56m consolidated vs 551,643.08m solo, a 0.50% difference; FY2014 236,608.40m consolidated vs 236,216.15m solo, 0.17%). The bank's subsidiaries (Southeast Bank Capital Services, SBL Exchange entities) are small relative to the bank, so the consolidated series tracks the solo series closely, but the basis differs from the Solo-basis series used for banks elsewhere in this dataset. Total liabilities are calculated as total assets minus total shareholders' equity (the source tables do not print a total-liabilities line). Net interest income is calculated as Interest Income minus Interest Expenses. Provision for Loans is calculated as Provision for Unclassified Loans plus Provision for Classified Loans. Total Expenditure is Interest Expenses plus Operating (Non-Interest) Expenses throughout. The composition of Total Income changes with the table format: for FY2011-FY2019 Investment Income is a separate line that sits alongside Non-Interest Income, so Total Income = Interest Income + Investment Income + Non-Interest Income (verified: FY2014 19,233.89 + 5,274.27 + 3,158.93 = 27,667.09 = reported Total Income; FY2019 26,258.88 + 5,288.98 + 4,844.75 = 36,392.61 = reported Total Income). From FY2020 the tables fold Investment Income into Non-Interest Income, so Total Income = Interest Income + Non-Interest Income (verified: FY2023 22,047.89 + 14,118.37 = 36,166.26 = reported Total Income). Investment Income is still reported separately in both formats and is retained as its own line item here, so for FY2020 onwards it must not be added to Non-Interest Income when reconstructing Total Income. Two data errors in the source table are flagged rather than silently corrected: (a) Paid up Capital is printed as 8,732.60 in the \"Key Financial Data and Ratios\" table but as 8,732.86 in the same report's \"10 Years at a Glance\" table and in the FY2016 report — 8,732.86 is used here as the corroborated figure, which leaves a 0.26m rounding gap against the table's Reserve Fund & Surplus of 11,051.52; (b) the Capital Surplus/(Deficit) row prints 18,032.77, which is exactly 10% of the FY2012 risk-weighted assets (180,327.66) — i.e. the required-capital figure was printed in the surplus row — so capital_surplus_deficit is omitted for FY2012 rather than recorded wrongly. The same erroneous 18,032.77 is repeated in the FY2016 report.",
